  "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix, every solution of the system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={\u03a6}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector\u00a0}c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Evaluating the identity $x(t)={\u03a6}_{0}(t)\\,c$ at $t=0$ gives \n\n$$\nx(0)={\u03a6}_{0}(0)c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{evaluating both sides at\u00a0}t=0.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Substituting ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ from $\\text{L1}$ and $c=x(0)$ from $\\text{L5}$ into $x(t)={\u03a6}_{0}(t)\\,c,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and substitution from $\\text{L1}\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}\\text{L5}.$",
